DEEP[22]=("<i>Yadṛcchā-lābha-santuṣṭaḥ</i>, “content with what comes by itself”. <i>Dvandvātītaḥ</i>, “beyond the pairs of opposites”. <i>Vimatsaraḥ</i>, “free from envy”. <i>Samaḥ siddhāv asiddhau ca</i>, “even in success and failure”.",
 "The six inner enemies, <i>ṣaḍ-ripu</i>, of Indian ethics are desire, anger, greed, delusion, pride and envy (<i>kāma, krodha, lobha, moha, mada, mātsarya</i>). This verse names envy directly. Commentators note how envy poisons action: one cannot work freely while comparing oneself to others. Śaṅkara reads the verse as describing a renunciant who lives on whatever comes. Rāmānuja applies it to the karma yogi in any walk of life. The pairs of opposites, heat and cold, praise and blame, recall 2.14 and 2.45. The verse gathers the karma yogi’s marks into one line.",
 "Notice envy when it rises. It is a signal that you have started measuring your life by someone else’s.")
DEEP[23]=("<i>Gata-saṅgasya muktasya</i>, “of one free from attachment, liberated”. <i>Jñānāvasthita-cetasaḥ</i>, “whose mind is established in knowledge”. <i>Yajñāyācarataḥ karma</i>, “doing action for yajña”. <i>Samagraṃ pravilīyate</i>, “all of it dissolves”.",
 "<i>Pravilīyate</i>, “melts away entirely”, is a striking image: karma dissolves like salt in water. Commentators connect this to 3.9, where action for yajña does not bind. Śaṅkara says the knower’s actions leave no trace because there is no doer behind them. Rāmānuja says actions done as worship of the Lord do not bind. This verse leads directly into the famous 4.24, which shows how every element of action can be seen as Brahman.",
 "Offer your work, and its weight lightens. What is offered is no longer a burden to carry.")
DEEP[24]=("<i>Brahmārpaṇaṃ brahma havir</i>, “the offering is Brahman, the oblation is Brahman”. <i>Brahmāgnau brahmaṇā hutam</i>, “offered by Brahman into the fire of Brahman”. <i>Brahmaiva tena gantavyaṃ</i>, “Brahman alone will be reached by him”. <i>Brahma-karma-samādhinā</i>, “absorbed in Brahman as action”.",
 "This verse is recited before meals in many Hindu homes, schools and monasteries, often followed by 15.14. Commentators read it at different depths. Śaṅkara: for the knower, every part of the act is seen as Brahman; there is nothing else. Rāmānuja: the whole act, as the Lord’s body, is pervaded by him; seeing this makes every act worship. Madhva: everything belongs to and is controlled by Viṣṇu. The word Brahman appears five times in one verse. It is a powerful meditation: the eater, the food, the fire of digestion and the goal are all the Divine.",
 "Before your next meal, try this verse, or simply the thought behind it. Food becomes an offering, and eating becomes worship.")
DEEP[25]=("<i>Daivam evāpare yajñaṃ</i>, “some offer yajña to the gods”. <i>Yoginaḥ paryupāsate</i>, “yogis perform”. <i>Brahmāgnāv apare yajñaṃ</i>, “others into the fire of Brahman”. <i>Yajñenaivopajuhvati</i>, “offer the yajña by the yajña itself”.",
 "Verses 4.25–30 list twelve kinds of yajña, showing that offering is far wider than fire ritual. Commentators find the second half difficult. Śaṅkara reads it as offering the individual self into Brahman, the knowledge that “I am Brahman”. Rāmānuja reads it as offering ritual itself with the understanding that it is the Lord’s worship. The list moves from outer to inner: ritual, sense control, breath, food, study, knowledge. Each is valid in its place. The Gītā’s generosity is clear: there are many ways to live sacredly.",
 "Your way of offering may differ from someone else’s. What matters is that something is offered, not hoarded.")
DEEP[26]=("<i>Śrotrādīnīndriyāṇy anye</i>, “others, the senses beginning with hearing”. <i>Saṃyamāgniṣu juhvati</i>, “offer into the fires of restraint”. <i>Śabdādīn viṣayān anye</i>, “others, sound and other objects”. <i>Indriyāgniṣu juhvati</i>, “offer into the fires of the senses”.",
 "Commentators explain two contrasting practices. The first is restraint: the senses are withdrawn, as in fasting or silence. The second is mindful enjoyment: the objects of sense are received without attachment, as an offering into the senses. Śaṅkara links the first to the <i>brahmacārin</i> or renunciant, the second to the householder who enjoys rightly. Rāmānuja reads both as steps in self-control. Many modern teachers find the second especially useful: listening to music, eating, or looking at nature with full attention and gratitude, rather than grasping.",
 "Enjoying something fully and gratefully, without clinging, can itself be an offering.")
DEEP[27]=("<i>Sarvāṇīndriya-karmāṇi</i>, “all the actions of the senses”. <i>Prāṇa-karmāṇi cāpare</i>, “and of the life-breath, others”. <i>Ātma-saṃyama-yogāgnau</i>, “into the fire of the yoga of self-control”. <i>Juhvati jñāna-dīpite</i>, “lit by knowledge”.",
 "The verse points to deep meditation, where the activity of the senses and even the vital functions are gathered into stillness. Commentators note the phrase <i>jñāna-dīpite</i>, “kindled by knowledge”: self-control is a fire, but it needs the spark of understanding. Without it, control is dull suppression. Śaṅkara relates this to the meditation on the Self. Rāmānuja to the yoga of chapter 6. In Patañjali’s Yoga Sūtra, the higher limbs, <i>dhāraṇā, dhyāna, samādhi</i>, describe similar inward gathering.",
 "Self-control works best when we understand why. Lit by understanding, discipline becomes a joy rather than a strain.")
DEEP[28]=("<i>Dravya-yajñās tapo-yajñāḥ</i>, “offering of things, offering of austerity”. <i>Yoga-yajñās tathāpare</i>, “offering of yoga, others”. <i>Svādhyāya-jñāna-yajñāś ca</i>, “offering of study and knowledge”. <i>Yatayaḥ saṃśita-vratāḥ</i>, “ascetics of firm vows”.",
 "<i>Dravya-yajña</i>, offering of wealth or things, includes charity, building wells, feeding the poor. <i>Tapo-yajña</i>, austerity, includes fasting and vows. <i>Yoga-yajña</i>, practice of yoga. <i>Svādhyāya</i>, study of scripture, especially chanting the Vedas. Commentators note that each of these is a yajña only when done as an offering. Charity for fame, fasting for show, or study for argument are not yajña. Chapter 17 (17.11–22) distinguishes the sāttvika, rājasika and tāmasika forms of sacrifice, austerity and charity.",
 "Giving, discipline, practice and study become sacred when done as offerings. Check the spirit behind each.")
DEEP[29]=("<i>Apāne juhvati prāṇaṃ</i>, “offer the in-breath into the out-breath”. <i>Prāṇe’pānaṃ tathāpare</i>, “and the out-breath into the in-breath, others”. <i>Prāṇāpāna-gatī ruddhvā</i>, “restraining the course of both”. <i>Prāṇāyāma-parāyaṇāḥ</i>, “devoted to breath control”.",
 "This is among the earliest clear references to <i>prāṇāyāma</i>. The breaths are named differently in different texts. Commentators here generally take <i>prāṇa</i> as the outgoing and <i>apāna</i> as the incoming breath, or the reverse. The three phases are <i>pūraka</i>, filling; <i>recaka</i>, emptying; and <i>kumbhaka</i>, holding. Patañjali’s Yoga Sūtra (2.49–53) describes prāṇāyāma as the fourth limb of yoga, which makes the mind fit for concentration. The Gītā mentions it again in 5.27 and 8.10–12. Breath, in Indian thought, is the bridge between body and mind.",
 "When stressed, try a few slow breaths, a little longer out than in. It is the simplest yoga there is.")
DEEP[30]=("<i>Apare niyatāhārāḥ</i>, “others, regulated in food”. <i>Prāṇān prāṇeṣu juhvati</i>, “offer breaths into breaths”. <i>Sarve’py ete yajña-vidaḥ</i>, “all these know yajña”. <i>Yajña-kṣapita-kalmaṣāḥ</i>, “their sins destroyed by yajña”.",
 "<i>Niyatāhāra</i>, regulated or moderate eating, returns in 6.16–17, where yoga is said to be not for one who eats too much or too little. Commentators explain “offering breaths into breaths” as strengthening the vital forces by controlled diet. The second half sums up the list: all these people, with all their different practices, know the meaning of yajña. Śaṅkara says the variety reflects different temperaments. Rāmānuja says each is a step toward the vision of the self. The Gītā consistently honours variety in practice while pointing to one goal.",
 "Eating with moderation and care is a quiet daily discipline. It is also one of the easiest places to begin.")
DEEP[31]=("<i>Yajña-śiṣṭāmṛta-bhujaḥ</i>, “eating the nectar left from yajña”. <i>Yānti brahma sanātanam</i>, “they go to the eternal Brahman”. <i>Nāyaṃ loko’sty ayajñasya</i>, “not this world for one without yajña”. <i>Kuto’nyaḥ kuru-sattama</i>, “how then the other, best of the Kurus?”.",
 "This verse echoes 3.13, where the good eat what remains after offering. Commentators note the upgrade: here the remnant is called <i>amṛta</i>, nectar, the drink of immortality. Śaṅkara says the remnant of offering purifies and leads to Brahman. Rāmānuja says it nourishes the practice that leads to liberation. The second half is pointed: even ordinary happiness in this world depends on giving and sharing; one who never gives cannot find lasting happiness here, let alone hereafter.",
 "Notice how food shared tastes better than food hoarded. The principle extends to time, money and attention.")
DEEP[32]=("<i>Evaṃ bahu-vidhā yajñā</i>, “thus many kinds of yajña”. <i>Vitatā brahmaṇo mukhe</i>, “are spread out at the mouth of Brahman”. <i>Karmajān viddhi tān sarvān</i>, “know them all as born of action”. <i>Evaṃ jñātvā vimokṣyase</i>, “knowing thus, you will be freed”.",
 "<i>Brahmaṇo mukhe</i>, “at the mouth of Brahman”, is read by Śaṅkara as “in the Vedas”, which describe these sacrifices. Others read it as “in the presence of the Lord”. Commentators differ on the conclusion. Śaṅkara: knowing that all these are actions of body, speech and mind, not of the Self, frees one. Rāmānuja: knowing that all these are means requiring action, and doing them rightly, frees one. Both agree the many yajñas share a common root and a common goal.",
 "Many paths, one principle: offering what we have and are. Find yours and walk it sincerely.")
DEEP[33]=("<i>Śreyān dravya-mayād yajñāj</i>, “better than sacrifice made of things”. <i>Jñāna-yajñaḥ parantapa</i>, “is the sacrifice of knowledge, scorcher of foes”. <i>Sarvaṃ karmākhilaṃ pārtha</i>, “all action without exception, Pārtha”. <i>Jñāne parisamāpyate</i>, “culminates in knowledge”.",
 "<i>Jñāna-yajña</i>, the sacrifice of knowledge, returns in 9.15 and 18.70, where studying the Gītā itself is called a jñāna-yajña. Commentators agree that knowledge is the goal of all action, but differ on what that means. Śaṅkara: knowledge replaces action; once one knows, action falls away. Rāmānuja: knowledge crowns action; the knower acts with understanding. Madhva: knowledge of God leads to devotion. Many modern teachers read this verse as an encouragement to study, reflection and understanding as forms of worship.",
 "Learning deeply is itself an offering. Time spent understanding is never wasted.")
DEEP[34]=("<i>Tad viddhi praṇipātena</i>, “learn it by bowing down”; <i>paripraśnena</i>, “by thorough questioning”; <i>sevayā</i>, “by service”. <i>Upadekṣyanti te jñānaṃ jñāninas tattva-darśinaḥ</i>, “the wise, seers of truth, will teach you knowledge”.",
 "This verse is the Gītā’s classic description of how to learn from a guru. Commentators note the three steps. <i>Praṇipāta</i> is humility, bowing down physically and inwardly. <i>Paripraśna</i> is questioning thoroughly, not doubting idly. <i>Sevā</i> is service, which builds the relationship and purifies the student. The teacher has two qualities: <i>jñānin</i>, learned, and <i>tattva-darśin</i>, one who has seen the truth. The Muṇḍaka Upaniṣad (1.2.12) says the seeker should approach a teacher who is <i>śrotriya</i>, learned in scripture, and <i>brahma-niṣṭha</i>, established in Brahman. The Gītā uses two parallel words.",
 "Find a teacher who lives what they teach. Approach with humility, ask honestly, and give something back.")
DEEP[35]=("<i>Yaj jñātvā na punar moham</i>, “knowing which, not again delusion”. <i>Evaṃ yāsyasi pāṇḍava</i>, “will you fall into, son of Pāṇḍu”. <i>Yena bhūtāny aśeṣeṇa</i>, “by which all beings without exception”. <i>Drakṣyasy ātmany atho mayi</i>, “you will see in your Self, and then in me”.",
 "This verse promises the end of the delusion of chapter 1: Arjuna will no longer see “mine” and “theirs”. Commentators read the vision differently. Śaṅkara: all beings in the Self, and the Self as one with the Lord, a vision of non-duality. Rāmānuja: all beings as like oneself in nature, and all as the body of the Lord. The Īśā Upaniṣad (verse 6) says: one who sees all beings in the Self and the Self in all beings does not hate. The Gītā develops this in 6.29–31.",
 "When we see others as part of ourselves, harming them becomes impossible. That is the root of real kindness.")
DEEP[36]=("<i>Api ced asi pāpebhyaḥ</i>, “even if you are, among sinners”. <i>Sarvebhyaḥ pāpa-kṛttamaḥ</i>, “the worst of all evil-doers”. <i>Sarvaṃ jñāna-plavenaiva</i>, “by the raft of knowledge alone”. <i>Vṛjinaṃ santariṣyasi</i>, “you will cross all evil”.",
 "<i>Plava</i>, a raft or boat. The image of crossing the ocean of saṃsāra is central in Indian thought; the Lord is often called the boatman. Commentators stress that the verse offers hope, not licence. Śaṅkara says knowledge destroys the very sense of doership on which sin depends. Rāmānuja says the knowledge of the self washes away all past wrong. The bhakti traditions pair this verse with 9.30–31, where even a great wrongdoer who turns to the Lord with undivided devotion becomes righteous. The Gītā consistently refuses to write anyone off.",
 "No past is too dark to step out of. The way out begins with understanding and a decision to change.")
DEEP[37]=("<i>Yathaidhāṃsi samiddho’gnir</i>, “as a kindled fire, firewood”. <i>Bhasmasāt kurute’rjuna</i>, “turns to ashes, Arjuna”. <i>Jñānāgniḥ sarva-karmāṇi</i>, “the fire of knowledge, all karmas”. <i>Bhasmasāt kurute tathā</i>, “turns to ashes likewise”.",
 "Commentators distinguish three kinds of karma. <i>Sañcita</i> is accumulated karma not yet bearing fruit. <i>Āgāmī</i> is new karma being created. <i>Prārabdha</i> is the karma that has already begun to bear fruit and produced the present body. Knowledge burns the first two. Prārabdha continues until the body falls, as an arrow already shot keeps flying, or a potter’s wheel keeps turning after the push. Śaṅkara explains this in detail. It is why a realised person still lives, eats and ages, yet is free.",
 "Understanding can change how we relate to our whole past. What once haunted us can become ash.")
DEEP[38]=("<i>Na hi jñānena sadṛśaṃ</i>, “nothing like knowledge”. <i>Pavitram iha vidyate</i>, “purifies here”. <i>Tat svayaṃ yoga-saṃsiddhaḥ</i>, “that, one perfected in yoga himself”. <i>Kālenātmani vindati</i>, “finds in time within himself”.",
 "<i>Pavitra</i>, purifier, is used of holy rivers, sacred grass, mantras. Commentators say knowledge outranks them all because it removes the root of impurity, ignorance itself. The phrase <i>kālena</i>, “in time”, is important. Knowledge is not a sudden gift without preparation; it ripens through practice. <i>Svayam</i> and <i>ātmani</i>, “oneself” and “within oneself”, stress that it is discovered inside, not borrowed. Śaṅkara says practice of karma yoga prepares the mind, and knowledge dawns by itself. Rāmānuja agrees that yoga leads to self-knowledge.",
 "Some understanding cannot be rushed. Keep practising; it will arrive when the ground is ready.")
DEEP[39]=("<i>Śraddhāvāṃl labhate jñānaṃ</i>, “the one with faith gains knowledge”. <i>Tat-paraḥ saṃyatendriyaḥ</i>, “devoted to it, senses controlled”. <i>Jñānaṃ labdhvā parāṃ śāntim</i>, “gaining knowledge, supreme peace”. <i>Acireṇādhigacchati</i>, “soon attains”.",
 "<i>Śraddhā</i> is a rich word. Śaṅkara defines it as trust in the words of scripture and teacher, based on understanding, not blind belief. The Taittirīya Upaniṣad (1.11.3) says <i>śraddhayā deyam</i>, “give with faith”. Chapter 17 explores three kinds of faith. Commentators note the three conditions here: faith, dedication and self-control. Faith opens the door, dedication keeps one walking, self-control keeps one from straying. The fruit, <i>parā śānti</i>, supreme peace, is the same peace promised in 2.71 and 18.62.",
 "Faith is what keeps us practising long enough to see results. Give a good practice that chance.")
DEEP[40]=("<i>Ajñaś cāśraddadhānaś ca</i>, “the ignorant and faithless”. <i>Saṃśayātmā vinaśyati</i>, “the doubting self perishes”. <i>Nāyaṃ loko’sti na paro</i>, “neither this world nor the next”. <i>Na sukhaṃ saṃśayātmanaḥ</i>, “nor happiness for the doubter”.",
 "<i>Saṃśayātmā</i>, “one whose very self is doubt”, describes a person paralysed by indecision. Commentators distinguish this from healthy questioning. Arjuna himself asks many questions; Kṛṣṇa praises inquiry in 4.34. The problem is doubt that never resolves, that refuses every answer. Śaṅkara says such a person loses this world (no success) and the next (no merit). Many readers find the verse psychologically sharp: chronic doubt makes commitment, and therefore happiness, impossible.",
 "Doubt is useful when it leads somewhere. When it only circles, choose, act, and learn from the result.")
DEEP[41]=("<i>Yoga-sannyasta-karmāṇaṃ</i>, “who has renounced action through yoga”. <i>Jñāna-sañchinna-saṃśayam</i>, “whose doubts are cut by knowledge”. <i>Ātmavantaṃ na karmāṇi</i>, “self-possessed, actions do not”. <i>Nibadhnanti dhanañjaya</i>, “bind, Dhanañjaya”.",
 "Commentators see this verse as joining the two threads of chapter 4: yoga (offering action) and knowledge (cutting doubt). <i>Ātmavān</i>, self-possessed, means steady in oneself. Śaṅkara reads “renounced through yoga” as renouncing the sense of doership. Rāmānuja reads it as offering all actions to the Lord. Either way, action without ego, guided by clear knowledge, does not bind. The chapter’s title, the yoga of knowledge and the renunciation of action, is summed up here.",
 "Offer the work, clear the doubt, stay centred. That is the whole chapter in three steps.")
DEEP[42]=("<i>Tasmād ajñāna-sambhūtaṃ</i>, “therefore, born of ignorance”. <i>Hṛt-sthaṃ jñānāsinātmanaḥ</i>, “dwelling in the heart, with the sword of knowledge”. <i>Chittvainaṃ saṃśayaṃ yogam</i>, “cutting this doubt, to yoga”. <i>Ātiṣṭhottiṣṭha bhārata</i>, “resort; stand up, Bhārata!”.",
 "<i>Jñānāsi</i>, the sword of knowledge, is a vivid image. Commentators note that the doubt is <i>hṛt-stha</i>, seated in the heart, not just in the head: Arjuna’s confusion is emotional as well as intellectual. The double command <i>ātiṣṭha</i> (resort to yoga) and <i>uttiṣṭha</i> (stand up) ends the chapter with energy. Śaṅkara says the doubt is about whether to act or renounce. Rāmānuja says it is about the nature of the self. Chapter 5 will address the doubt between renunciation and action directly. The colophon calls chapter 4 Jñāna-Karma-Sannyāsa Yoga.",
 "Some doubts live in the heart, not the head. Cut them with clear understanding, and then act.")
